
1. Before you touch anything, SIGINT already has a handler
--------------------------------------------------------------
signal.getsignal(signal.SIGINT) ......... <built-in function default_int_handler>
...is signal.default_int_handler ........ True
SIGINT's number ......................... 2

It is an ordinary callable, so we can just call it ourselves:
    it raised ........................... KeyboardInterrupt

So KeyboardInterrupt is not what Ctrl-C does. It is what THIS function
does, and this function is replaceable.

2. Replacing it
--------------------------------------------------------------
signal.signal() returns the OLD handler . <built-in function default_int_handler>
...which is the one from section 1 ...... True
keep it, and you can put it back.

3. A real SIGINT, delivered to ourselves
--------------------------------------------------------------
signal.raise_signal(signal.SIGINT) is what the terminal does when you
press Ctrl-C, minus the terminal:

    I got a SIGINT, but I am not stopping

...and the next line still runs. Nothing was raised, nothing unwound.
A handler that returns normally resumes the code it interrupted, which
is why a `while True:` loop keeps counting.

4. What the handler is handed, and the name that shadows a module
--------------------------------------------------------------
arg 1, signum ........................... 2 (SIGINT)
arg 2, a frame object ................... frame
        it was executing ................ <module>

The second parameter is a FRAME: the line that was running when the
signal arrived. Name it `time` and the module is gone inside the handler:

    time.sleep(0) inside it ............. AttributeError

The loop outside keeps working, because its `time` is the global one.
The trap is local to the handler, and only springs if the handler ever
needs the module -- to time a shutdown, say.

5. A signal that arrives in the middle of time.sleep()
--------------------------------------------------------------
an alarm was set for 0.05s into a 0.25s sleep
the handler ran ......................... True
time.sleep() raised ..................... False
the sleep still lasted its full 0.25s ... True

PEP 475 (Python 3.5): a syscall interrupted by a signal is RETRIED
with the time that was left, once the handler returns. Before 3.5 the
sleep returned early and you had to loop around it yourself.

6. Putting the default back
--------------------------------------------------------------
signal.signal(SIGINT, signal.default_int_handler)

the same raise_signal now ............... KeyboardInterrupt

Three things can go in that second argument:
    a function of yours ................. Python calls it
    signal.SIG_DFL ...................... the kernel's default: die
    signal.SIG_IGN ...................... thrown away, not delivered

SIG_DFL for SIGINT kills the process without a traceback and without
running `finally:` blocks. default_int_handler is what gives you the
traceback -- it is Python's politeness, not the kernel's.

7. The counter that overwrites itself: "\r" and when it reaches the screen
--------------------------------------------------------------
print("\r{}".format(i), end="") rewinds to the left margin and prints
over what is already there, so one line counts up instead of a thousand
lines scrolling. Whether you SEE it count depends on buffering:

line_buffering=True  (a terminal)
    after writing "41" .................. b''
    after writing "\r" .................. b'41\r'
line_buffering=False (a pipe)
    after writing "41" .................. b''
    after writing "\r" .................. b''

A line-buffered stream flushes on \r as well as on \n -- which is the
only reason that counter animates without flush=True. Down a pipe there
is no line buffering at all and the whole count arrives at exit.

This run's own stdout:
    sys.stdout.isatty() ................. False
    sys.stdout.line_buffering ........... False
    (the recorded key is captured, so both read False;
     run this in a terminal and both flip to True)

8. Two rules about threads
--------------------------------------------------------------
signal.signal() from another thread ..... ValueError
a SIGINT raised by the worker ran in ..... MainThread

Only the main thread may install a handler, and every handler runs in
the main thread -- whichever thread the kernel happened to deliver to.
A worker blocked on a socket does not get interrupted; the main thread
gets the callback, and has to tell the worker itself.
